---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/foundational/money-inflation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Money is a technology with three jobs: a <strong>medium of exchange</strong> (no more
    bartering chickens for shoes — imagine finding a shoemaker who wants exactly your chicken,
    at the same time, in tradeable portions: the "double coincidence of wants" that barter
    makes mandatory), a <strong>unit of account</strong> (one yardstick so thousands of prices
    can be compared in your head) and a <strong>store of value</strong> (savings that keep
    their meaning between this month and next year). Inflation is the third job going wrong:
    money quietly losing weight each year, so the same note buys less.
  </p>
  <p>
    The plot below shows the damage compounding does. At 3% inflation, money's purchasing
    power halves in about 23 years — slow enough to ignore for a while, fast enough to
    transform a retirement. Drag the inflation rate upward and the curve steepens brutally:
    compounding is indifferent to whether it is building your savings or dissolving them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the slow tax — 3% inflation halves money's worth in about 23 years; drag i upward and watch it collapse" widget="plotter" config={powCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>The rule of 70</strong> converts any growth rate into a halving (or doubling) time
    by division: 70 ÷ 3 ≈ 23 years, 70 ÷ 10 = 7 years, 70 ÷ 50 = 17 <em>months</em>. It works
    because 70 ≈ 100 × ln 2, the algebra of any compounding process — so the same trick
    estimates how fast prices double, how fast a population grows, and how fast an investment
    doubles at 7% interest (70 ÷ 7 = 10 years). One tool, any compounder.
  </p>

  <h2>What money is, and who makes it</h2>
  <p>
    Money has taken whatever form carried trust: salt and cowrie shells, then coins whose
    metal <em>was</em> the value, then paper notes <em>backed</em> by gold, and finally today's
    <strong>fiat money</strong> — paper and digits with no intrinsic worth, valuable because
    the state accepts them for taxes and everyone else follows. The gold backing ended for
    good in 1971, when the US suspended dollar–gold convertibility; since then all major money
    is a claim on trust, which is exactly why inflation control became a central bank's core
    job. And the "printing press" is stranger than it sounds: commercial banks create most of
    the money supply themselves, every time they make a loan — your new mortgage deposits
    money that did not exist before, to be destroyed when the loan is repaid. The central bank
    (the Federal Reserve, the Bank of England, the ECB) sits above this system: it issues
    base money, sets the interest rate banks lend to each other at, and acts as
    <em>lender of last resort</em> in panics — the institutional response to the lesson that
    bank runs are self-fulfilling prophecies.
  </p>

  <h2>Inflation: measuring a moving floor</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Inflation</strong> is a sustained rise in the <em>general</em> price level — not
    one price, and not one jump. It is measured by statistical offices that survey household
    spending, build a weighted "basket" of hundreds of goods, and track its total cost as the
    Consumer Price Index; the inflation rate is the index's annual percentage change. The
    distinction that organises everything else is <strong>nominal versus real</strong>: a 5%
    pay rise during 7% inflation is a real pay <em>cut</em>, and a savings account paying 4%
    during 6% inflation loses 2% of its real value every year (real rate ≈ nominal rate −
    inflation). Inflation therefore redistributes by surprise: borrowers win (they repay in
    cheaper money), fixed-income savers and pensioners lose, and wage earners lose whenever
    wages lag prices — which is why union contracts, tax brackets and even bus fares get
    "indexed" to the index itself. On top of the redistribution sit pure inefficiencies:
    <em>shoe-leather costs</em> (energy spent dodging cash) and <em>menu costs</em> (the
    literal and digital cost of repricing everything).
  </p>
  <p>
    Why do most central banks target ~2% rather than zero? Three considered reasons: a small
    inflation buffer makes <em>deflation</em> less likely (see below — it is the more
    dangerous disease); modest inflation greases wage adjustments, since cutting a named wage
    is psychologically harder than failing to raise it; and price indices slightly overstate
    true inflation (they miss quality improvements), so a measured 2% is close to a real 1%.
    The target is a design choice, not a law of nature.
  </p>

  <h2>Why prices rise</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Demand-pull inflation</strong> is too much spending chasing too few goods — an
    economy pushed beyond its capacity, whether by war finance, stimulus cheques meeting
    snarled supply chains (2021), or a credit boom. <strong>Cost-push inflation</strong> is
    inputs getting dearer: the 1973 oil embargo saw crude roughly quadruple in months, and
    because energy feeds into nearly everything, the price level followed. A
    <strong>wage–price spiral</strong> can entrench either: workers demand raises to catch
    rising prices, firms raise prices to cover rising wages, and each validates the other.
    <strong>Money growth</strong> is the deep mechanism: the quantity theory (MV = PY, money
    stock times velocity equals prices times output) says that if money grows much faster
    than real output, the adjustment must come through prices. Milton Friedman's summary —
    "inflation is always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon" — is the proposition the
    hyperinflations below test to destruction. And <strong>expectations</strong> tie it
    together: once people <em>expect</em> 10% inflation, wages and prices pre-emptively rise
    by 10%, making it true. This is why central banks guard their credibility so jealously —
    an anchor on expectations does half the disinflation work in advance.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="US inflation by year — the Great Inflation, the Volcker cure, the Great Moderation, and the 2022 spike" widget="chart" config={cpiCfg} />

  <p>
    The chart is the century's monetary history in one line. The <strong>Great Inflation</strong>
    of the 1970s peaked at 13.5% (1980) after oil shocks and loose policy; Fed chair Paul
    Volcker broke it by pushing interest rates toward 20% — deliberately causing a sharp
    recession in 1981–82, the price of credibility restored. The two decades after were so
    calm economists called it the <strong>Great Moderation</strong>; the 2021–22 spike then
    looked at first like classic demand-pull (pandemic stimulus) amplified by cost-push
    (energy after Russia's invasion of Ukraine, and shipping chaos), and how much of it would
    <em>persist</em> was the live question of the decade.
  </p>

  <h2>The central bank's lever: monetary policy</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Monetary policy</strong> is the central bank steering demand by moving its
    interest rate. Raise the rate and the transmission runs: borrowing becomes dearer →
    mortgages, car loans and business investment shrink → spending cools → demand-pull
    pressure on prices eases → inflation falls, at the cost of slower growth and higher
    unemployment. The arithmetic is felt personally: a 300,000 mortgage goes from about
    15,000 a year in interest at 3% (interest-only) to about 18,000 at 6% — across a whole
    economy, that difference is demand withdrawn. Cut the rate and every arrow reverses.
    Two design lessons hardened over the last fifty years: central banks work best
    <strong>independent</strong> of election calendars (governments face a temptation to
    stimulate before votes and disinflate after — the "political business cycle"), and
    policy has limits — at the "zero lower bound" rates cannot fall much further (hence the
    unconventional <em>quantitative easing</em>: buying bonds to push down longer-term
    rates), and a cost-push shock can't be cured by demand squeezes without extra pain.
  </p>

  <h2>When it goes catastrophically wrong</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Hyperinflation</strong> — conventionally, prices rising over 50% per
    <em>month</em> (Cagan's 1956 threshold) — is the rule of 70 with the accelerator jammed,
    and the mechanism is always the same: a government spending far more than it taxes
    (usually after war) covers the gap by printing, prices respond, spending buys less so
    more printing is needed, and the loop feeds on itself. Weimar Germany, 1923: prices
    doubled roughly every 3–4 days, the dollar traded at 4.2 <em>trillion</em> marks, and
    workers were paid twice daily to shop before lunch. Hungary, July 1946: doubling about
    every 15 hours — the fastest ever recorded. Zimbabwe, November 2008: an 89.7
    sextillion-percent annualised rate at peak, prices rising ~80 billion percent in a
    month, and a 100-trillion-dollar note that bought little. The pattern's end state is
    money ceasing to be money: barter, foreign dollars and cigarette-currency take over,
    and stabilisation requires a new currency plus — the real cure — a credible end to
    deficit financing. The opposite trap, <strong>deflation</strong>, looks virtuous and
    isn't: falling prices reward waiting (why buy today?), spending stalls, and debts grow
    <em>heavier</em> in real terms while incomes shrink — a spiral Japan spent the 1990s and
    2000s unable to fully escape. Between the two diseases, central banks have learned that
    a steady, boring, credible 2% is not timidity; it is the point.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final word on the thermometer itself, because the measurement is a design choice with
    consequences. Basket weights come from spending surveys and are rebased regularly
    (housing costs are the eternal argument: some indices include them, headline CPI does
    not), quality change is adjusted away — a phone that costs the same but does twice as
    much counts as a price <em>fall</em> under hedonic adjustment — and substitution is
    partly built in, since shoppers flee to cheaper stores when prices rise. Each choice is
    defensible and each is contested, because the index is not just a statistic: it indexes
    pensions, rents, tax brackets and inflation-linked government bonds, letting savers hold
    inflation-proof assets and letting economists read the market's inflation forecast
    directly off the price gap between indexed and ordinary bonds. Measuring money's worth
    is itself a small act of monetary policy.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked ledger: the shrinking dollar</h2>
  <p>
    Run the numbers on the curve in fig. 1 and they stop being abstract. US consumer prices
    rose roughly eight-fold between 1970 and 2024, so the dollar in a 1970 Christmas card
    bought what about eight dollars buy today — a movie ticket at 1.55 dollars then is
    15-plus now; the federal minimum wage went from 1.60 dollars an hour to 7.25, and the
    honest comparison is that its <em>real</em> value fell. The rule of 70 cross-checks:
    prices at 3% double every ~23 years, so two doublings and a bit over 54 years ≈ ×4–5 —
    but average US inflation in that period ran closer to 4% (doubling every ~18 years),
    giving three doublings ≈ ×8. ✓ Now the personal finance version: a savings account
    paying 5% during 4% inflation grows your money at roughly 1% real — the bank's headline
    number is mostly inflation passing through. This is why wage negotiations, pension
    indexation and long contracts are all, underneath, arguments about this one percentage
    point: every price in the economy is a real value wearing a nominal costume, and
    inflation is the rate at which the costume changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two ideas carry the subject into the macro course. The <strong>Phillips curve</strong>
    (1958) observed that low unemployment came with higher wage inflation — for two decades
    read as a menu (choose your mix of inflation and joblessness) until the 1970s delivered
    <em>stagflation</em> — high inflation <em>and</em> high unemployment together — breaking
    the simple trade-off and forcing expectations into the model: only <em>surprise</em>
    inflation buys jobs, and surprises cannot last. And the <strong>anchor</strong> has since
    become measurable: bond markets publish inflation expectations directly (the gap between
    ordinary and inflation-indexed government bond yields), so a central bank's credibility
    is quoted daily, in basis points. Beyond the syllabus, the frontier questions are live:
    why inflation stayed low for thirty years (globalisation? demographics? central bank
    skill — the "missing inflation" puzzle), whether the 2022 episode was transitory (the
    word's most costly adjective), and whether crypto-assets with fixed supply would make
    good fiat replacements — which runs headlong into the observation that a little measured
    flexibility, not monetary hard-coding, is what the post-1971 system has been engineered
    to buy.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cagan, "The Monetary Dynamics of Hyperinflation" (1956); Friedman, "The Role of Monetary Policy" (1968); Phillips, Economica (1958); Federal Reserve and Bank of England inflation histories and targets; OpenStax, <em>Principles of Economics</em>; Cambridge IGCSE Economics 0455 syllabus, sections 3.1, 4.3 and 4.7.</p>
</TopicPage>
